A||N||6||Heisenhund||Temp gauge probs||||5||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||05-01-2000||10:50 PM||||Ok, I've finally gotten another Fiero after being without one since 1987. Boy, is my wife ticked! :-&gt; I think she thinks that I still hold it against her that she wrecked my last one.<P>Anyway, I am now the proud owner of an 86 GT. The temperature guage goes to the high peg after a short run (say 2 miles). Then it sways around up in the red regions for awhile then drops down & finally settles in at an indicated 150 to 200F. Since I had to replace the engine in my 84 due to overheating, this concerns me greatly. Any suggestions on some likely suspects?<P>Thanks<BR>Heis<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-01-2000||11:23 PM||||Are you sure that it's accurate?  I mean, if it's getting that hot I wouldn't even drive it.  Are you sure the coolant is full?  Checke to make sure the thermostat isn't sticking(if you're not sure, just replace it cuz it's cheap.)  I went with a 180 deg in my '87 and and it never gets anywhere near hot.  Make sure the pressure cap doesn't leak at all while running(had the same problem... caused some major heat issues.) Check the timing... If it's way off, the engine will run much hotter!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Heisenhund||05-02-2000||09:22 AM||||Ok, you addressed most of the same questions I have. Stat's been replaced already, making a tester unit to test the gauge. Hadn't thought about the timing, though. Wouldn't bad timing show up in other areas (rough idle, loss of power at certain speeds, etc.)? Thermostat cap looks new, radiator cap might need replacing. Do you happen to have a part number for the 180 stat?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-02-2000||10:25 AM||||not handy but it's a standard Stant part available anywhere.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||05-02-2000||10:45 AM||||Check and make sure that you do not have air in the system. That will cause it to overheat and eventuaaly come back down. That reading would be false as the reason it shows overheating is the hot air and steam in that spot.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Heisenhund||05-02-2000||05:27 PM||||Hmmm....."air in the system". That would fit the indicvations, provided the guage isn't bad.<BR>Is there another venting procedure other than that given in the manual?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Heisenhund||05-03-2000||09:10 AM||||OK, went with the 180F stat and now the gauge doesn't quite make it to the high peg...it still gets to 260F, then comes back down. I think that looks like it's either the gauge or the sender. In either case, I have to disconnect the bugger. All my attempts so far haven't worked. Is there a clip on the connector in some unseen place? Also, I have my calibration checker ready, but I was wondering if anyone knows some resistance to temperature values other than full scale. I was told a Pontiac service manual has a table of values. I really don't want to plunk down the $85 for this baby just yet. Any help?<BR>||||reg||1
